{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_1_guesses": "few", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_2_guesses": "few", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 2, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "For both puzzle one and two, I made a small mistake that then allowed me to make some incorrect deductions that were tricky to trace back to. ", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "Again, I made some small mistakes when I assumed that some boxes could be crossed out with an \"x\". This one I found a little trickier because it had more \"1\" boxes, which have more spaces that they can fit. ", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "I think this time I learned from the previous puzzles not to cross anything out too quickly which helped my boxes be more accurate and led to fewer mistakes that needed fixing.", "strategy": "First I would look for the rows with larger numbers or more number to fill out the rows. Once that was filled in, it made it a lot easier to place corresponding values for the perpendicular row/columns because I was able to determine roughly where some of the boxes should be filled in. I also made sure to cross out any boxes on either side of a \"completed\" section so that I wouldn't accidentally add more later.", "comments": "I think generally puzzles with smaller numbers were harder to complete."}, "t": "2026-05-21T19:47:22.023775+00:00"}
